I got NetHack 3.1 off of ftp.uu.net, and had no problems extracting it.

Note that the tar file doesn't create its own subdirectory for
extracting the distribution tree of files.  To keep things clean, I'd
suggest creating a subdirectory first, and then untar'ing in there...

I've just read that a few people were having problems extracting this,
so for the sake of verification, here's what I got:

--------------------------
# ls -l nethack31.tar.Z
-rw-r--r--  1 root     2585536 Jan 28 05:59 nethack31.tar.Z
# sum nethack31.tar.Z
28943  2525
# md5 nethack31.tar.Z
eb7ff1fd6aa1f3a60cbd685825f74187 nethack31.tar.Z

Md5 is the RSA Data Security, Inc Message-Digest 5 algorithm.  (I'm
required by its license to describe it as such.)  It computes a 128 bit
checksum, which is currently computationally infeasible to forge.  I
have the source to it, if anyone wants it.
--------------------------

The build itself went perfectly fine for me, except for the standard
minor warnings about pointer type mismatches.   This was on a
NeXTstation running NeXTstep 3.0 and using the bundled compiler, gcc
version 1.93.

Wow...from what I've seen in the code and it the various data files (I
haven't really played enough to test things yet), NH3.1 is worth the
wait. 

Chocolate covered kudos to the Dev Team!!

Is anyone else out there interested in getting a NeXTstep interface
comparible to the X-windows interface built?  If so, let's get in touch,
as I'd be interested in grinding something like that out.  However, I
don't see this as being a high priority item, since the standard Unix
tty interface works perfectly fine.  I might first putz around a little
and maybe do something easier like adding code in to generate sound like
the PC & Mac versions....

